- F-57 **A numeral's type depends on the path and on the library in scope, and in generic code only the getters `x.zero` and `x.one` and the witness `typecase` give a `T` on both paths.** The witness `typecase` is a `typecase` over a function of type `() -> T`, with one clause for each number type (P-78). The checker types a numeral by its own numeral type, `NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]` or `RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]`, which the compiler's prelude and the one library each declare (`compiler/Types.java`, `useCompilerLibraries` and `useFortressLibraries`; F-79), and converts it into its default where nothing fixes a type (F-342). Where nothing fixes a type, walk gives an integer numeral its default by its magnitude, a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, which it prints by those names; where its place fixes a type, walk converts it (F-341). In code generic over `T extends Integral[\T\]`, `x + 1` fails the checker but runs under walk: no coercion from a numeral into the type parameter `T` itself can be declared, since such a coercion is refused as a cyclic hierarchy. The checker refuses a numeral outside a bounded number type's range, or rounding to an infinity, statically, and walk refuses an integer numeral out of range when it runs, at the place it does not fit (F-342, F-341). Detail: `perf-probes/prelude/distance-triage.md` section 2.3; `reviews/numerics-plan-coordinator/evidence-B.md` sections 3.1, 4.1 and 6.2, measured on an earlier library (F-79); `x.zero` by reading, not measured.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the refused coercion is one into the type parameter T, not one generic over a numeral's four numbers, which every number type now declares (Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:467-469, AnyIntegral's; P-174)

- F-118 **A numeral's value reaches the compiled code intact, and the compiled path prints a large `NN32` or `NN64` value unsigned.** A numeral that no place types becomes its default through that type's own coercion, which the checker inserts (`Functionals.scala`, `defaultNumeral`); only a numeral that the checker leaves without a coercion reaches the code generator's fallback, a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by exact bit length (`CodeGen.java`, `forIntLiteralExpr`), and which numerals do is not measured. A folded constant that the coercion desugarer types at a library number type is that type's constant (F-340), and a numeral as written at a number type is its type's coercion folded to a constant or made once for the place (F-347). So on the compiled path `4294967295` and `18446744073709551615` keep their values, as under walk, where they are a `ZZ64` and a `ZZ` (F-74, F-341); an untyped binding of the second is a `ZZ`, its default (F-342). The compiled path prints an `NN32` or `NN64` value above its type's signed half unsigned (F-313; test `compiler_tests/UnsignedPrintingCompiled`). Gated, for the numerals at `ZZ`, `ZZ64`, `NN32` and `NN64`, by `compiler_tests/IntLiteralWrapRepairR2`. Detail: `compile-ladder/repair-r2-literal-wrap/REPORT.md`, "The failure-mode question"; `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "1. Fidelity: the static numerals, path by path, against his design"; from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the bit-length default is the code generator's fallback for a numeral that the checker gave no coercion, its reach unmeasured (the review's correction B and section 1, "Every place where the library's types still take a way of their own", 3; Functionals.scala:1016-1028, CodeGen.java:4342-4360); "walk converts a numeral bound to NN32 or NN64" left to F-341; "and compares with 5 at ZZ", what remained of the removed comparison at ZZ64 (row 328, FIXED), cut

- F-262 **A numeral's sign does not reach its type on either path, so no overload on a numeral exponent's type can tell a positive exponent from a negative one, as in `x^16` and `x^(-16)`.** The numeral itself has no sign (`Specification/basic/lexical-structure.tex:1053-1080`). The checker types every integer numeral by its numeral type, `NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]`, whose static arguments hold no sign, under the compiler's prelude and the one library alike (`scala_src/typechecker/impls/Misc.scala:469-472`), before it converts it (F-342). Both paths fold `prefix -` on a numeral into a numeral node of negative value before the checker, and fold `^` of numerals by an integer numeral up to 4096 (F-339), but leave an integer base at a negative exponent: `10^16` is a numeral, and `10^(-16)` stays an operation whose exponent is a numeral (`compiler/desugarer/IntegerLiteralFoldingVisitor.java:315-325`). Walk's numeral is its default, an `Int` value read at `ZZ32` (F-74, F-341); a negated numeral at a program's own type is the type's own prefix `-` applied to the coercion's result for the numeral, on both paths (P-174; tests `tests/NumeralStaticDesignWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/NegativeNumeralOwnType.test`). That no overload can tell the two exponents apart is by reading. Detail: `reviews/integer-power-fable-judgement.md` section 9.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: "By reading." scoped to the overload claim, since two tests now gate the minus at a program's type

- F-341 **Walk's numeral is its default, carrying the numeral's four numbers, and converts from them where its place fixes a type.** An integer numeral is a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by its value, a radix-point numeral the `RR64` nearest its value, one value per site (`interpreter/evaluator/values/Numerals.java`). At a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter, a declared return type and a coercion it converts through the place type's coercion from `NaturalNumeral` or `RadixPointNumeral`, run with the four numbers bound as values of type `ZZ`, one result per site and type; a folded constant at a library number type converts from its exact value, outside the coercion; a numeral out of the type's range, at a type with no coercion from it, or outside its coercion's `where` clause is refused there when it runs; a store keeps its default alone. Dispatch fits it as it is at its default, then by its own type's coercions only, and reads a tie at the default; a type parameter that several numerals fix takes the narrowest default holding them all. So `q: QQ = 0.1` is 1/10 and `y: RR64 = 0.5` is `SEQV` a computed 0.5, but `h = 0.5` is `false` for `h: QQ = 1/2`, the library's `=` over `Any` fitting the numeral at its default `RR64` (row 973; observed once at `17e93a7da8`, no test pins it). Tests: `tests/NumeralTypesWalk.fss`, `NumeralOwnTypeWalk.fss`, `NumeralNoCoercionRefusedWalk.fss`, `NumeralAtReturnWalk.fss`, `NumeralWhereRefusedWalk.fss`, `NumeralFoldedConstantLibraryWalk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-types/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the batch moved here: a walk program on climb batch 29b's merged tree, 17e93a7da8, printed 'h = 0.5: false' (row 973); the code state stays in the entry as the observation's bound

- F-346 **Under walk the library's `=` and `=/=` over `Any` meet a numeral at its default, so a number and a numeral of one value answer both as if the values differed.** Under walk `u = 0` is `false` and `u =/= 0` is `true` for a zero `u` of `NN32`, `NN64` or `RR32`, and so are `q = 0.5` and `q =/= 0.5` for `q: QQ = 1/2`: with no `=` pairs between a numeral type and a number type (P-174), `=` takes the numeral at its default (row 973), and `opr =/=(a:Any, b:Any) = NOT (a=b)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:98`) follows it (row 966). So under walk `e =/= e'` still answers `NOT (e = e')`. The compiled path until the switch-over answers `false` for `u =/= 0` through the prelude's `=/=` on each integer type. Test: `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXNotEqualNumeral.fss` (an expected failure), with `XXXNumeralEqualsNumberWalk.fss` for `u = 0`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-literals-spec/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "11. Defects, each with its home".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "11. Defects, each with its home"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the title, kept from climb batch 29, said that =/= departs from NOT (e = e'), which its body showed false once the = pairs went; both now answer as if the values differed (rows 966 and 973 as rewritten); the opr-overview clause goes with the title

- F-347 **Every numeral's place on the compiled path whose type names no type parameter is one value made before the program runs: a JVM constant where the fold of its type's own numeral coercion ends in an `int`, `long`, `float` or `double`, else a static final filled once by the coercion's method of the four numbers as `ZZ` values.** The code generator folds the body of the place's type's coercion, the library's or a program's, over a fragment of constants, bound names, natives over the built-in types' representations and calls of functions and methods in the fragment (`compiler/codegen/NumeralCoercionFold.java`); the method, `coerce_T№natural` or `coerce_T№point`, is compiled once in the declaring component and called from any component, and a program type's minus on a negative constant runs once with it (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java`). A place whose type names a type parameter calls the method at each evaluation (row 957). A coercion whose body puts two calls of the program's own functions in one operator or one argument list, as `small(n) - small(m)` does, spawns a task, and the spawn fails the class's load (row 971); bind the operands to locals first. Under walk a folded constant at a library type converts from its exact value. Tests: `compiler/desugarer/NumeralCoercionFoldJUTest`, `library_tests/NumeralFoldIntegers`, `NumeralFoldFloats`, `compiler_tests/NumeralAcrossComponent`, `NumeralMinusOnceCompiled`, `NumeralCoercionOnceCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "The list for the record-landing workflow, one line each with its home"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the title limited to row 957's case, which the body names; row 971's reach widened by the review's finding 1 (ParallelismAnalyzer.java:44, :158-212; the coordinator's run after the landing, 'ClassCastException' at 1 and at 4 threads; 9fb5ab231f's message); CodeGen.java's method names left to the report, section 1: numeralCoercionSiteDesc, emitFoldedNumeral, numeralCoercionMethods, emitNumeralCoercionCall, negatedNumeralSite

- F-342 **The compiled checker converts a numeral where nothing fixes a type into its default, refuses one out of range, and refuses a numeral type written other than as a parameter's type.** Where no place's type has a coercion from the numeral's type (an untyped binding, `Any`, `Object`, `Number`, an unbounded type parameter, a receiver, a join, a result with no declared return type), a numeral becomes a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by its value, or an `RR64` with a radix point. A type parameter that numerals alone fix takes the narrowest default holding them all, and the conversion into the default ranks as no coercion. A numeral outside a bounded number type's range, or rounding to an infinity, is a static error, also as a tuple's element at a tuple type and in a result position of an `if`, a `case`, a `do` block or a `try`; a program's type with no coercion from the numeral's type refuses a numeral. `NaturalNumeral` and `RadixPointNumeral` are written in a program only as a parameter's type, never as a type that an object or a trait extends. A `nat` or `int` parameter read as a value is a `ZZ32`, but a `ZZ` where it is one of a numeral coercion's four numbers (`scala_src/typechecker/staticenv/KindEnv.scala`, `getType`). It builds P-173's checker, P-174's written-only rule and four numbers, and P-40's size value; a `typecase`'s selector keeps its own numeral type (row 963). Tests: `compiler_tests/LiteralDefaultCompiled`, `LiteralDefaultResultCompiled`, `XXXLiteralTypeUnwritable.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondZZ32Range.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondRangeInTuple.test`, `XXXNumeralJoinBeyondRange.test`, `XXXNumeralTypeExtendedRefused.test`, `NatValueZZ32Compiled`, `NumeralStaticDesignCompiled` (a `v` beyond `ZZ64`). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-checker/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-numeral-checker/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: the pass after climb batch 29b's landing: the second sentence, 1,048 characters, split at its semicolons into one sentence for each rule; the words unchanged

- F-345 **The specification states, in the revision form, the static numerals (P-174, P-176) and P-171's string literals.** The literals chapter gives a numeral a numeral type that exists only at compile time, `NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]` or `RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]` below a `Numeral` without static parameters, the four numbers its static arguments, naturals of any size, and every number type's coercion generic over the four numbers, read as `ZZ` and folded, one value per place. It also gives the conversion where a place fixes a type and the default elsewhere; a program's type receiving a numeral as written, its minus on the coercion's result, any other static expression through its coercion from the default's type; a `where` clause over the four numbers refusing statically; and the numeral types written only as parameter types (`Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex:98-285`). The constant chapter gives the fold, converted from its exact value at a library type (`constant.tex:80-126`). The numbers chapter gives the coercions, with no `=` pairs and no `asFloat` on a numeral type (`basic-lib/numbers.tex:47-71`). The binary chapter's numeral declarations are expressible now and left out. Appendix I gives `revival-numerals` and `revival-string-literals` (`Specification/appendices/changes.tex`), whose Effects name each departure of the two implementations with its row. Test: the specification's build (`ant genSource`, `ant tex`), 0 warnings by the skill's grep. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals-spec/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
