- F-131 **The one library's number tower is flat.** It builds P-43. `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR64`, `RR32` and the numerals' trait `Numeral` are siblings under `Number`; below `Numeral` lie the numeral types `NaturalNumeral` and `RadixPointNumeral`, which each of the eight converts from (F-79). The compiled checker gives a numeral its numeral type and converts it where it stands (F-342); walk gives it its default, `ZZ32`, or `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit, and converts it where its place fixes a type (F-341). Each wider integer type converts from each narrower one, and `RR64` from a `ZZ32`; a `ZZ64` goes into `RR64` only when written out (P-74). `QQ` converts from the five integer types, and `RR64` converts from `RR32` too (F-139). `NN32` does not yet convert into `RR64`: P-75 decides that it will. `Number` declares no `=`; each number type declares one at each number type it coerces from or into (F-324). Under walk `SUM <|1, 2, 3|>`, with no static argument, is 6, its list read at `ZZ32` (F-344), and an empty `SUM` over an additive group that is not a number dies (row 436). Gated by `tests/FlatTowerRungF.fss` for every type but `RR32` and the numeral types: that none is another's subtype, their coercions and `=`. `RR32`'s place is gated with F-139, and the numeral types' with F-79. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-flat-tower/REPORT.md` sections 5 and 17.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree: Numeral has no comprises clause since a26e74f5c8 (Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:457-465), and no = pairs a numeral type with a number type (P-174; F-79)

- F-324 **The one library's `Number` declares nothing; each number type declares its own `asFloat` and an `=` at each number type it coerces from or into.** It builds P-160's second step and P-165. `Number`'s `comprises` clause, `asFloat`, `=` and `exactValue` are struck (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:380-381`, `.fsi:288-289`); the nine types declare `asFloat` in their apis; 42 declarations give a pair of `=` for each of the 21 coercions, so two numbers of different types compare by value where a coercion joins them, and otherwise by the fallback `a SEQV b` or a written `asFloat(x) = y`; the vector norm is declared at each of the eight types; the reflective generator chooses among the five former listed types. A program's own number type compares by its own `=` or by the fallback. Gated by `tests/NumberOwnTypeNoAsFloatWalk`, `NumberEqualityOwnTypeWalk`, `NumberMixedEqualityWalk`, `VectorNormElementTypesWalk`, `NumberOwnTypeArrayWalk`, `NumberOpenTraitPlusBesideArrayWalk` and the team's `ReflectiveQuickCheckTest`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md`.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree: the numeral types, which every number type coerces from, have no = since a26e74f5c8 (P-174; F-79)

- F-180 **`import java` is wired on walk through `ForeignComponentWrapper` and `ClosureMaker`, but four gaps stop it and no test uses it.** In a probe, six small Java patches closed the five gaps the tree then had, two of them for gap 4. A component with two declarations bound through `import java` then printed on walk what the compiled path prints. Gap 1, the type table, is closed on the tree: in walk's world the three-argument `NamingCzar.fortressTypeForForeignJavaType`, which walk's repository reaches for a written `import java` (`repository/GraphRepository.java:957`), reads a Java type as the one library's (`compiler/NamingCzar.java:365-368`; F-298), so a component with one such import now stops at gap 3, 'Non-top-level reference to imported …' (row 845). P-58 chose another way: a `builtinPrimitive("…")` body whose string names a static Java method in `nativeHelpers/`. Its compiled half is built, the rewrite of such a body into a call before the type checker (F-298); so is walk's half, a loader of such a method beside the glue classes (F-299). Two findings bear on any binding that both paths share. First, a helper raises a Fortress exception by naming a class of the compiled prelude, such as `fortress.CompilerBuiltin$IntegerOverflow`. Walk has no such class: its loader of a `builtinPrimitive` helper translates the raise into the library's exception (F-299), but through a written `import java` walk ends the run with a `FortressImplementationError`, unless the probe's patch for gap 5 (`gap5-helper-raise.patch`) translates the raise. Second, by reading, a helper whose parameters or results are compiled-path classes, such as `FZZ32Vector`, cannot take walk's values. The probe's closure converts only Java's numbers, booleans, strings and `BigInteger`, and the helper classes had 50 such parameters or results when read once with `javap`, before `nativeHelpers/simpleIntLiteralArith.java` was deleted; walk's loader refuses at load a binding whose declared type no Java type carries (F-299). Detail: `perf-probes/prelude/natives-shape.md` section 1 (the gaps and walk's half); `perf-probes/prelude/natives-binding-probe.md` section 1 (the compiled half; its section 2 measures the `import java` form, which P-58 did not take); `compile-ladder/rung-natives-compiled/SKEPTIC.md`, "My programs, old against new".
