- F-301 **On the compiled path a native helper is called through a generated wrapper over the box classes, and once the loop is hot C2 inlines the whole path, so the call costs nothing of its own.** A binding `builtinPrimitive("pkg.Class.method")` and a written `import java` alias call compile to the same bytes: an `invokestatic` of `native.pkg.Class.method`, a class the compiled path writes into `nativewrapper_cache/`, which unboxes each argument with `getValue`, calls the helper and boxes its result with `make`, a new box each time (`compiler/nativeInterface/FortressMethodAdapter.java:303-327`; `compiler/runtimeValues/FZZ32.java:32-34`). A functional method such as `ZZ32`'s `+` reaches the wrapper through the dispatcher, an interface call, the springboard and the trait's static body. C2 inlines all of these frames, the helper, `make` and the box's constructor chain; only C1's profiling code refuses the helper and the chain's deep frames. Escape analysis then removes every temporary box, a helper's result passed into another helper among them, and one box stays per assignment to a mutable variable per iteration: 16 bytes for a `ZZ32`, 24 for an `RR64`. The library's form and the prelude's allocate the same. Observed once on one small program, `perf-probes/helper-calls/HelperCalls.fss`, by `javap`, C2's inlining log and the bytes that JFR counted, with no timing, on a machine shared at a load of 20 to 25; no test checks it. Detail: `perf-probes/helper-calls/REPORT.md` sections 2 to 5.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-302 **In those loops C2 inlines down to depth 12 from the loop, against the 16 at which it stops by default, and a temporary box whose constructor chain passes the limit is no longer removed.** At JDK 25's default `MaxInlineLevel=15` a frame at depth d inlines when d is at most 16. The prelude's `+` reaches `Object.<init>` under its result's box at depth 12, a top-level binding at 10. With the limit lowered to 8, a loop of two nested helper calls rose from 33 to 96 bytes per iteration, since its temporaries' chains no longer inlined. So an operator called from more than 4 further inlined frames keeps its temporary boxes. By reading, the one library's `ZZ32` springboard chain is two classes longer than the prelude's, `ZZ32`, `AnyIntegral`, `Number`, `AnyAdditiveGroup`, `Object`, `Any` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:775`, `:671`, `:380`, `:356`) against `ZZ32`, `Number`, `Object`, `Any` (`LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fsi:210`), since a springboard extends the springboard of its trait's first `extends` entry (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:5527-5534`). If `FZZ32` extends the one library's springboard after the switch-over, the margin is 2 frames; not measured, since nothing compiles against the one library yet. Observed once (F-301). Detail: `perf-probes/helper-calls/REPORT.md` sections 3 and 4.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-35 **The `FortressLibrary` api has 9 declarations with a dead size: a size parameter that occurs in none of the api declaration's parameter types or return type.** They are the three one-dimensional `subarray` methods, with `nat o`; the two-dimensional `subarray`, with `nat o0` and `nat o1`, and the three-dimensional one, with `nat o0` to `nat o2`; and the traits `Rank`, `Indexed1`, `Indexed2` and `Indexed3`, whose `nat n` occurs in no member's type but tells `Rank[\1\]` from `Rank[\2\]`, as `Rank1 extends Rank[\1\]` does (`FortressLibrary.fsi:1364`). The component's `subarray` bodies read their offsets as values. The api declarations check clean, since a size that reaches no type, name or value is no error (P-40). The census of 2026-09-21 found 25 and missed the three-dimensional `subarray`; the 17 vector and matrix operators have lost their sizes since (P-40). Where a component declares a function without the api's dead size, the export checker reports the api's declaration as missing, since it compares static-parameter lists by length, name and kind. Detail: `perf-probes/nat/REPORT.md` sections 2 (the 25, by line) and 7 (the export checker).
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-44 **The compiled checker enforces the multiple instantiation exclusion rule and relies on it, and the one library meets the rule.** The rule: no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait. The checker's Return Type Rule relies on it: with the rule relaxed alone, a program passes the checker and dies with `IncompatibleClassChangeError` (`OverloadingOracle.scala:81-105`; the probe `ProbeMIEPick`). The team's JVM type mapping relies on it too: no closure of extends lists names one generic at two static arguments, so the class that template expansion makes for an instantiation carries the type relations over as Java ones (`Papers/Implementation/TypeMapping.tex:125`, read). F-48 gives the kinds of static argument that the rule compares. Walk applies no instantiation exclusion: it accepts a type that extends two instantiations, and its overloading check does not count them as excluding (row 416; the review's section 3). P-43 keeps the rule. Besides the flat number tower of P-43, the library meets it by three changes of `952892a00`: `TotalComparison` extends `Comparison` and `StandardMinMax[\TotalComparison\]`, no longer `StandardTotalOrder[\TotalComparison\]`; `AnyMaybe` extends `AnyUniqueItem` alone, no longer `Equality[\AnyMaybe\]` beside it; and `andCondCombine` chooses `andRelCond` by a `typecase`, in place of a second `ANDCOND`. So under walk, a program's `p1 ANDCOND p2` of two relational predicates, conditions built from a relation over a generator, which `filter` fuses, is no longer itself a relational predicate (row 458). The distance holds no exclusion error (F-53). Detail: `reviews/multiple-instantiation-exclusion.md` sections 1 to 3; `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:122-124` (`TotalComparison`), `:1064` (`AnyMaybe`); `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:5087-5095` (`andCondCombine`).
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-58 **When measured, on an earlier library, microGPT's flat program, C4, had 26 errors of its own under the compiled checker, and none came from a numeral's type.** 15 were arrays without sizes: `Array[\RR64,ZZ32\]` where the library wants `Vector`, `Matrix` or `Array3`. 5 were numeral powers such as `10.0^(-5)`, refused as ambiguous because two declarations of `^` each needed the integer converted (row 533). 6 were small slips. On a copy patched with decision D's diff, which sizes the model's arrays (P-87), 14 remained: the 5 powers, 3 `DOT` calls that failed on the api's unused sizes, 1 bridge, `adam`'s, that passes arrays sized only at run time, and 5 small slips. Since then the 17 vector and matrix operators' unused sizes are gone (F-35), so by reading the 3 `DOT` refusals are cleared. Since then too, `MultiplicativeRing`'s `^` takes an `AnyIntegral` exponent, and `RR64` declares `^(self, b:AnyIntegral)` beside its `^(self, b:Number)`, the component's header (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:285`, `:365-366`). The distance stage's driver checks a component of `10.0^(-5)` and `10.0^(-8)` against the interpreter's library with no error, so the tie row 533 names does not reproduce there. Each count is a floor, since the checker stops a block at its first failing statement (row 474). Detail: `reviews/array-design-ways.md` section 2; `compile-ladder/rung-integer-power/REPORT.md` section 7; `compile-ladder/rung-export-last/SKEPTIC.md` section 3.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation re-anchored on the landed tree: RR64's two ^ declarations are at FortressLibrary.fsi:365-366; the cited :369-370 named sin and cos already at climb batch 29b's base (b5b0f82c1), where the two were at :367-368

- F-63 **The one library's api declares `String`'s `split` and `splitWithOffsets` once, abstract; redeclares no `zip` in `List`, where the inherited `ZeroIndexed.zip` serves; has `Condition.map` return `Condition[\G\]`, the component's type; and closes `QQ` by `comprises { ... }`.** `Condition` is the sequential generator trait at `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:1017`. In their earlier forms, the pair declared twice and `List`'s `zip[\F\](other: List[\F\])`, the checker refused the api (measured). The other repairs tried, the plain pair kept once and a `zip` narrowed to `ZeroIndexed`, read the same count; the forms kept follow the specification's export rules (the report's Decisions). The `Condition.map` error was seen only under a checker change that did not land; otherwise it is read from the export check's comparison of return types, and no test gates that edit. `QQ`'s clause lists no type, since its one value type `Ratio` is private to the component, and an api may leave out a private type only by `...` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:398`). No api-declared type extends `QQ`, so F-64's refusal, which falls on such a type, does not arise. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md`, "Summary", "Decisions" and "Defects and their homes".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-74 **Under walk, an integer numeral where nothing fixes a type takes its default by its value: a `ZZ32` if it fits 32 bits, a `ZZ64` if it fits 64 bits, a `ZZ` above that (P-173).** Where a place fixes a type, walk converts the numeral from its four numbers (F-79, F-341). Its run-time type is the library's value object `Int`, `Long` or `BigNum`, below `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` and `ZZ` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fsi:126`, `:129`, `:196`). Walk reads it at its trait where it infers a static argument, so `Box(3)` is a `Box[\ZZ32\]` and `<|1, 2, 3|>` an `ArrayList[\ZZ32\]` (F-344). A size used as a value is a `ZZ32` up to 2^31-1 and a `ZZ64` above, where P-173 makes it a `ZZ32` (F-140; row 962). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-types/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `reviews/numeral-switch-judgement.md` sections 2 and 3, a probe of an earlier design that gave every integer numeral a type of its own.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-78 **The compiled checker instantiates a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes at the intersection of its upper bounds, never `BottomType`, and tries a call by subtyping without the expected type first.** It builds P-48 and P-49. Where the call has an expected type, its attempts come in this order: subtyping without the expected type, kept when its result converts to it; subtyping with the expected type; coercion with it; and coercion without it. The second lets a call whose untyped function argument only the expected type fixes type-check, since the coercion attempt cannot check such an argument (row 507). P-49 decides the first and the third; the second and the fourth are an agent's choice, the instance-bound rung's (its decision record, D4). An argument of intersection type fixes a parameter at the whole intersection, not at one conjunct (row 541). The run time has no class for an intersection type, so such an instance dies as it loads (row 559). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*` and `InferContextKeepsFit`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 7; the order, by reading, `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/decision-record.md`, D4, and `scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala:781-785`.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-85 **In the interpreter's library, the rung's repairs of the generator, condition, reduction and nested-generator declarations that the compiled checker refused are in forms that the library already uses. `__bigOperator2`'s fused branch passes its result through `cast[\O0\]`.** That branch, the `typecase` branch that runs a nested big operator through `generate2`, casts its result to `O0` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1585`), the library's device for an arm whose type the `typecase` cannot show (F-264), so the checker no longer types its body `OR(I0,O0)` where `O0` is declared (row 632) and no site of these declarations is on the distance (F-53). Each method body of `Condition` that calls `cond` writes the call's result type as its static argument, such as `cond[\Maybe[\G\]\]`. The sites that the rung left were cleared by other work: F-250 holds the identity functions' `cast` and the typed `embiggen`, F-93 how library code reads a generator's size and index (row 629), and F-144 how the reductions without an identity are typed at their element type (row 628). Gated by `tests/GeneratorDeclarations.fss` and `tests/NestedReductionFusion.fss`, which run the arm fused, and the cast by the distance stage. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-generator-slips/REPORT.md` sections 5 and 8; `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/REPORT.md` section 9.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-268 **The code generator emits `Any` as a static argument in an `extends` clause, but crashes on a tuple static argument that holds `Any`.** It emits `GETSTATIC fortress/AnyType$Any$RTTIc.ONLY` (`CodeGen.java:6250-6255`). For `Holder[\(Any, ZZ32)\]` it stops with 'Cannot invoke "com.sun.fortress.nodes.IdOrOp.getApiName()" because "id_or_op" is null' (row 889). Test: `compiler_tests/ExtendsGenericAtAnyCompiled.test`, `XXXExtendsGenericAtTupleOfAny.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-walls/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed, and why" and "5. Programs run old against new, and the probes".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-276 **String's four concatenation families are three top-level operators each, as juxtaposition's are; with the known fixes beside them, the full check of symbolic operators loses the library's 54 known-fix errors.** `||`, `|||`, `//` and `///` are declared at `(Any, String)`, `(String, String)` and `(String, Any)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2671-2690`); the `(String, String)` `||` reaches `FlatString`'s and `Concatenable`'s own `||` by `typecase`, which String's api states (`Library/String.fsi:17-19`, `:29-31`), so under walk `"ab" || "cd"` stays a `FlatString` of depth 0. The known fixes beside them: `EmptyString`'s meet, `Integral`'s and `ZZ`'s `=`, String's api header extending `DelegatedIndexed`, `Maybe`'s `|self|` and subscript meets, `Float`'s `|self| : RR64` and `^` meet, and the api's six vector and matrix `+` and `-` out. With these alone on climb batch 21's base the full check went 191 to 137, the 54 of F-245 gone and none come; the stock count stayed 3 and the distance 5. Under walk, a program's own trait with a functional `||`, `|||`, `//` or `///` that can meet `String` needs `excludes { String }` or the meet, where walk analyses the library afresh (row 342). Tests: `tests/StringConcatenationFamiliesWalk.fss`; the distance stage, which checks every operator (F-281); `tests/XXXFunctionalMethodMeetOperator.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-symbolic-library/REPORT.md` sections 1, 3 and 4.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-279 **Under walk an `Array2` or `Array3` based above zero slices like a 0-based one, except a shifted `Array2`.** `Array2.subarray` and `Array3.subarray` hand their 0-based views the offset less the base, and `Array3`'s `[:]` passes its base, as `Array1`'s do (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2914`, `:3316`, `:3295`; `:2626`). `SubArray2`'s own `subarray` subtracts the output base instead of its own (`:2998`, row 808), so a slice of a shifted `Array2` still reads the wrong elements, and a shift of a slice, a column, a row or a shifted `Array2` ends in Java's `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Tests: `Array2SliceAtNonzeroBase`, `Array3SliceAtNonzeroBase`, `XXXArray2ShiftedSlice`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-slices-list-equality/REPORT.md`, sections 1 and 5.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-285 **The interpreter's library writes the static argument of every clause form whose big operator's bound names its parameter, and of every list comprehension, in the twelve components the distance checks; `__bigOperator2`'s fused arm casts its result to `O0`.** The library follows P-78's workaround for row 731: `BIG MIN[\ZZ32\]` in `String`'s `upto` and `beyond` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4580`, `:4586`), and `<|[\E\] self[i] | i <- r |>` in `List`'s range subscript (`Library/List.fss:151`), beside `SUM[\T\]` at `:2719`, `:3105`, `:3110`, `SUM[\ZZ32\]` at `:4764`, `:4781`, and the team's `transpose` (`List.fss:504`). The clause forms left unwritten are of plain-bounded big operators (`BIG ||`, `BIG AND`, `BIG OR`), which the checker takes at their bound, or of `BIG LEXICO`, which has no static parameter. The fused arm (`:1585`) uses the library's cast device for an arm whose type the `typecase` cannot show (F-264), as `__builtinFactory1`'s `N[\0\]` arm does (`:2739`). So the distance holds no site of rows 731 and 632, while row 731's checker gap stays for user programs and the team demos. Under walk, the written comprehension makes `List`'s strided subscript answer a `List[\E\]`; `PureList`'s, at `Library/PureList.fss:73`, answers a list of objects at their elements' run-time types, and a list of `ZZ32` or `String` at its element type (row 824). Tests: the distance stage (`explorations/coordinator/tools/distance/run.sh`, its typecheck kind 0); `tests/WrittenReductionArgumentsWalk`; `tests/NestedReductionFusion`; `tests/PureListStridedSubscriptWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/REPORT.md`, sections 3, 5, 7 and 9.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations of Library/FortressLibrary.fss that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3); the sort named :4639 and :4645, and the five bare lines after List.fss:151 and the two of the fused and N[\0\] arms are FortressLibrary.fss's too, read on the landed tree

- F-286 **The api's `String` writes `abstract` on every member that its component's trait leaves without a body, and every string object defines them.** `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2592-2667` writes 14 of `String`'s members `abstract`: `size`, `depth`, `asFlatString`, `isBalanced`, `verify`, `asDebugStringIndented`, `get`, `uncheckedSubstring`, `rangeContains`, `splitWithOffsets`, `split`, `balanced`, `opr ||(self, c:Char)` and `writeOn`. The component's trait (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4511-4647`) declares all 14, none with a body. `FlatString`, `CatString`, `EmptyString` and `SubString` each define all 14 under walk. `String`'s `excludes` clause is `{ Number, Char, AnyMultiplicativeRing }` on both sides; `AnyList` states its exclusion of `String` itself (`Library/List.fsi:55`). So `String` has no entry in the `FortressLibrary` export error (F-282). `CatString`'s `rangeContains` answers what its flat halves answer, so it carries row 607's miss. Gated by `ProjectFortress/tests/StringMembersPromisedWalk.fss` and by the distance stage's per-site list. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-members/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2 and 5.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-303 **Walk carries a declared `Float` in a Java `double`, as it carries `RR64`, so a helper binding with a parameter or result declared `Float` loads; 57 of `Float`'s 62 bindings, and the top-level `fromRawBits`, name `simpleDoubleArith`'s helpers, and every value they give under walk is the glue classes'.** `javaFor` names `Float` beside `RR64` (`interpreter/glue/StaticNativeApp.java:64`); without it walk refused such a binding at load, 'no Java type carries the declared type of parameter a.' The 57 are `asString`, `asExprString`, `|self|`, the five comparisons, unary `-`, `+`, `-`, `DOT`, `TIMES`, juxtaposition, `/`, `SQRT`, `^` at a `Float` and at an `RR64` exponent, the eight elementary functions, `floor`, `ceiling`, `truncate` and `round`, over helpers that existed; and `isNaN`, `isInfinite`, `rawBits`, `nextUp`, `nextDown`, `narrow`, `=/=`, `MIN`, `MAX` and the 18 directed roundings, over 27 new one-line helpers, with `fromRawBits` over a 28th (`LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:61-206`; `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4705`; `nativeHelpers/simpleDoubleArith.java:136-246`). Its other 5 have no fitting helper and still name glue classes: `^` at `RR32` and `AnyIntegral`, `atan2`, and the floor and ceiling brackets `|\self/|` and `|/self\|` (`LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:163-201`). F-299 counts the bindings that name a helper. Test: `tests/NativeHelperFloatCarriedWalk.fss`, with `tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatWalk.fss`, `tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatBitsWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatCompiled` and `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatBitsCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-float/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-natives-float-bits/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-305 **`CHOOSE` on `ZZ32` and `ZZ64` names its helper on both paths, and the helper is exact, raises `IntegerOverflow` where the binomial coefficient does not fit, and shares no state between calls.** It builds P-73. Trait `ZZ32`'s and trait `ZZ64`'s `CHOOSE` name `simpleIntArith.intOverflowingChoose` and `simpleLongArith.longOverflowingChoose` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:844`, `:953`), which the compiler's prelude binds too, `NN32`'s compiled `CHOOSE` through the `ZZ64` helper; `NN32`'s binding in the one library names `simpleUnsignedIntArith.unsignedIntOverflowingChoose` through the `NN32` transfer (F-313). Past their safe-bound tables every coefficient overflows, from `34 CHOOSE 17` on `ZZ32` and `68 CHOOSE 34` on `ZZ64`, and the helpers raise `IntegerOverflow` there (`nativeHelpers/simpleIntArith.java:222-225`, `simpleLongArith.java:200-203`); each slow-path call has its own scratch array (`simpleIntArith.java:174-175`, `simpleLongArith.java:153-154`), so calls in parallel no longer corrupt each other. Swept against the exact value, 64,649,240 `ZZ64` pairs and 6,023,925 `ZZ32` pairs give walk's glue arithmetic. Test: `tests/ChooseHelperWalk.fss`, `tests/ChooseHelperZZ64Walk.fss`, `compiler_tests/ChooseHelperCompiled`, `compiler_tests/ChooseHelperZZ64Compiled`, `compiler_tests/ChooseTableCompiledRungE`; the guards `tests/ChooseZZ32Walk.fss` and `tests/ChooseZZ64Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-choose/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2 and 5.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-306 **Under walk, trait `ZZ64`'s 26 and trait `ZZ`'s 22 native bindings name helpers of `nativeHelpers/`, keep every value the glue classes gave, and their `DIV` by zero throws `DivisionByZero`.** The 48 (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:877-984`, `:1088-1194`) name 29 helpers of `simpleLongArith`, `simpleIntArith`, `simpleUnsignedLongArith` and `simpleArbitraryPrecisionArith`, those that the compiler's prelude binds for the same operations, and 11 new ones, four of `REM` and `MOD` (F-312) and seven of the divisors, `ZZ`'s `CHOOSE` and `cmp` (F-314); `ZZ64`'s four wrapping bindings name the wrapping helpers that the prelude binds under its `BOX` spellings (row 348). `longOverflowingDiv` and `div` raise the library's `DivisionByZero` under walk (F-24); `REM` and `MOD` name the new helpers, and raise `DivisionByZero` at a zero divisor too (F-312). A `ZZ` helper takes only a `BigNum` value, since walk's loader unboxes a `BigInteger` by `getBigInteger`; walk's coercions convert another integer operand first. Test: `tests/NativeHelperFamilyIntegersWalk.fss`, `tests/ZZ64DivisionByZeroWalk.fss`, `tests/ZZDivisionByZeroWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyIntegersCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-integers/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored to trait ZZ64 and trait ZZ, each to its end (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-307 **The small rest's eight native bindings name their helpers of `nativeHelpers/`, and walk's values are unchanged: `Long`'s and `BigNum`'s `asString` and `asExprString`, `NN32`'s and `NN64`'s `signed`, `Char`'s `codePoint` and the top-level `char`.** They are at `LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:389`, `:391`, `:524`, `:545`, `:547`, `:595` and `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1079`, `:4710`; the four over `NN32`, `NN64` and `Char` name the special names that the checker reads at those types (`compiler/NamingCzar.java:369-380`). `signed` above the signed half wraps, as a low-bits name does (F-331), and `char` keeps a code point outside 0 to 1114111, as the glue classes did (row 857). The top-level `printlnWithThread` and `getProperty` are natives over `FlatString` on helpers, beside overloads over `String` that flatten (F-299). Test: `tests/NativeHelperFamilySmallWalk.fss` and `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilySmallCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-small/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-311 **`Int`'s, `RR32`'s, `NN32`'s, `Long`'s and `BigNum`'s `asFloat` answer `RR64`, as each type's own `asFloat` declares in its api (F-324), and name `nativeHelpers`' `intToDouble`, `floatToDouble`, `unsignedIntToDouble`, `longToDouble` and `toDouble`; walk's values are unchanged.** The specification: `numbers.tex:61`. At `Float`, the team's declared result, the checker refused the move ('Function body has type RR64, but declared return type is Float.'); walk carries both in a `double` and answers the same `Float` object (F-303). `longToDouble` and `toDouble` are new, `(double) a` and `a.doubleValue()`. Tests: `tests/NativeHelperFamilyAsFloatWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyAsFloatCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-asfloat/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-312 **`REM` and `MOD` on `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ` name helpers of `nativeHelpers/` on both paths, keep every value the glue classes gave, and throw `DivisionByZero` at a zero divisor.** The ten bindings (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:836-839`, `:945-948`, `:1048-1051`, `:1163-1166`; `ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:493-496`) name `simpleIntArith.intRem` and `intMod`, `simpleLongArith.longRem` and `longMod`, `simpleUnsignedIntArith.unsignedIntRem` and `unsignedIntMod`, `simpleUnsignedLongArith.unsignedLongRem` and `unsignedLongMod` (through the transfers of F-308), and `simpleArbitraryPrecisionArith.rem` and `mod`: `REM` the remainder of the truncating division, `MOD` of the floor division with the divisor's sign, and on the natural numbers `MOD` the same as `REM`. Each raises `fortress.CompilerBuiltin$DivisionByZero` at a zero divisor, which walk raises as the library's `DivisionByZero` (F-24). 626 printed pairs are the old code's. Test: `tests/ZZ32RemainderByZeroWalk`, `ZZ64RemainderByZeroWalk`, `NN32RemainderByZeroWalk`, `NN64RemainderByZeroWalk`, `ZZRemainderByZeroWalk`, `NativeHelperFamilyRemainderWalk`; `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyRemainderCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-remainders/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-315 **The code generator compiles a trait or object that extends a generic trait at `Any`, a `try` with a `forbid` clause, and a function or a method declared `test`.** An exception of a listed type that no catch clause took is thrown again as a `ForbiddenException` chained to it, made by the library's own constructor, and the `finally` clause does not run (row 890). The code generator's guard of modifiers, which functions and methods share, admits `test` (`CodeGen.java:3029-3031`, read in the code); a method declared `test` compiled and ran in one probe, and no test gates it. `Bottom`, `Self` or an abbreviated type as that static argument, a tuple that holds `Any` (row 889), and the `override` modifier (row 650) are still refused. The library's `test stringifyChars` stops next at a varargs call (row 624). F-34 gives what the code generator still refuses in the library. Tests: `compiler_tests/ExtendsGenericAtAnyCompiled.test`, `ForbidClauseCompiled.test`, `TestModifierCompiled.test`; for row 890, `XXXFinallyOnEscape.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-walls/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2 and 5; `reviews/batch-25-review.md`, "Where the text, the brief or the principles split", 3 and 4.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-321 **Against the interpreter's prelude the checker reads the whole helper class that a binding names, so a binding of a helper of `nativeHelpers/systemOps` fails it.** `systemOps.getArgs` answers an `FStringVector` (`nativeHelpers/systemOps.java:24`), which `NamingCzar` maps to `FortressBuiltin.StringVector` (`compiler/NamingCzar.java:337-338`), a type that the interpreter's `FortressBuiltin` does not declare. So a component whose binding names any `systemOps` helper stops: 'FortressBuiltin.StringVector is undefined.' A binding passes the checker only where every method of its helper class maps to the one library's types; `FlatString`'s line separator names `stringOps.lineSeparator` for this reason. No binding of the one library names `systemOps`: `System`'s `getEnvironment` and `getProperty` name `stringOps`, and its `args` names `programArgs` (F-337). `CompilerSystem` still imports `systemOps`' `getArgs` and `getProperty` (`Library/CompilerSystem.fss:13-14`). Checked alone on `ee14222d`, the checker over `System` against the one library reports no error, '### rc=0'; neither stage of the gate reads `System`. No test checks it: the compiled tests check against the compiler's prelude, which declares `StringVector`. Shown by the distance stage with `Library/FlatString.fss`'s `natLineSeparator` naming `systemOps.lineSeparator`: 'FortressBuiltin.StringVector is undefined.', 'File FlatString.fss has 1 error.' Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-natives/REPORT.md`, "A site that came, and went"; `compile-ladder/rung-system-natives/REPORT.md`, "The checker over System".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-327 **The integers' shifts and powers over `AnyIntegral` convert their count or exponent in Fortress and call public bare bindings at narrow headers.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ` keep `LSHIFT`, `RSHIFT` and `^` over `AnyIntegral` (and `ZZ`'s `shift`) as plain Fortress around a public bare `builtinPrimitive` binding at a `ZZ32` count (`ZZ32`, `NN32`, `ZZ`) or a `ZZ64` count (`ZZ64`, `NN64`) and a `ZZ32` exponent, declared in the api; private functions `shiftCount32`, `shiftCount64`, `powerCount32` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:729-774`) and `countNN32`, `exponentNN32` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:399-425`) keep `Int.shiftCount`'s saturation and `Int.powCount`'s sign and parity, a `ZZ` count or exponent clamped in `ZZ` and then read by `.asZZ32`. Ten new helpers and five existing; every value of the walk guard is the glue's. Meaning: the 16 bindings count among F-299's 374, a `ZZ32` count or exponent reaches its bare binding directly, and P-69 and P-153 hold on both paths at these declarations. Test: `tests/NativeHelperShiftPowerWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyShiftPowerCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-shift-power/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-330 **Walk carries `FortressLibrary`'s `JavaBufferedWriter` in a Java `FortressBufferedWriter`, and `stdOut`, `stdErr`, `Writer` and `BufferedWriter` are plain Fortress objects over it.** The trait's `write`s, `flush` and `close` and its three factories, over a file name, standard output and standard error, are natives on `nativeHelpers/fileOps` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1987-1996`, `:2009-2014`); walk's loader carries the trait by its name through `FortressBuiltin`'s private native object `JavaBufferedWriterObject` (`interpreter/glue/StaticNativeApp.java:70`), and the checker's walk-world table reads `FortressBufferedWriter` and `FortressBufferedReader` as the one library's traits (`compiler/NamingCzar.java:486-487`). A missing file raises the library's `FileNotFoundException`, which a `catch` sees. `FileWriteStream` is a plain `WriteStream` over `JavaBufferedWriter` too, with its `flush` (F-332). Test: `tests/NativeHelperWriterWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyWriterCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-io-writer/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-332 **Walk carries `FortressLibrary`'s `JavaBufferedReader` in a Java `FortressBufferedReader`, and `FileReadStream`, `Reader`, `stdIn` and `FileWriteStream` are plain Fortress objects over the Java-backed traits `JavaBufferedReader` and `JavaBufferedWriter`.** The trait's eight methods and its two factories, over a file name and for standard input, are natives on `nativeHelpers/fileOps` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1998-2018`); walk's loader carries the trait by its name through `FortressBuiltin`'s private native object `JavaBufferedReaderObject` (`interpreter/glue/StaticNativeApp.java:71`). `File` and `Reader` are plain components, and each object holds its Java-backed value in its field `under`. `uncheckedReadChar` answers -1 at the end by the reader's `eof`, since `jbrRead` answers 0 there. `FileWriteStream` is a `WriteStream` with its `flush`. A missing file raises `FileNotFoundException`, and a read after `consume` raises `IOFailure`. A `catch` sees each on both paths, since a compiled helper's raise builds the prelude's exception (F-336). F-299 counts the bindings. Tests: `tests/NativeHelperReaderWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyReaderCompiled`, `compiler_tests/FileOpsRaiseCaughtCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-io-reader/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

- F-334 **The compiled checker orders a call's candidates, a coercion's candidates and a trait's ancestors by a linear extension of their partial order, read once a pair, so no call stops it at 32 candidates or more.** `scala_src/useful/PartialOrder.scala` gives `minimal` and `linear`, used at `Functionals.scala:761`, `:771-776`, `:809-828`, `CoercionOracle.scala:350-369` and `Formula.scala:610`; a tie among the most specific keeps the first minimal candidate in the call's order of overloadings, which follows the build as well as the program (row 935). On the one library the checker's results are those of the sort it replaced, and the distance checks `value object Char` to its end (`#crash none`). Tests: `compiler_tests/PartialOrderCandidatesCompiled`, `compiler_tests/TieAmongMostSpecificCompiled`, `PartialOrderJUTest`, the distance stage. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-partial-order/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2, 6 and 8.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored (the review's correction B; PLAN.md:762, review.3)

<!-- C51 to C61: POSITIONS sentences that the tree made false, under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31. C58, P-154: nothing, its citation is pinned to the team's commit a874948ac. -->

- P-173 **A numeral converts where it stands: where its place fixes a type, into that type at compile time from its exact value; where nothing fixes one, into the machine default, a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit, and with a radix point an `RR64`.** A place fixes a type when its declared type has a coercion from the numeral type, as the eight number types have and a program's own type may (P-174); `Any`, `Object`, `Number`, an unbounded type parameter and no declared type take the default. So `x = 3` is a `ZZ32`, and `x SEQV z` is `true` for `z: ZZ32 = 3`; `bigN = 3000000000` is a `ZZ64` at its binding (row 946). The default applies to a numeral, a literal after the fold, never to an expression of a numeral type, and a `nat` parameter used as a value is a `ZZ32` where it is a size (P-40), and a `ZZ` where it is one of a numeral type's four numbers (P-174). A type parameter that several numerals fix takes the narrowest default that holds every value, an `RR64` where any has a radix point, each numeral converting into it exactly; a float numeral whose conversions into `QQ` and `RR64` tie reads `RR64`. At a typed place the conversion is exact or rounds once (P-76, P-172), and a numeral outside the type's range is refused statically; walk converts from the exact value at the same places, place by place, before it runs the checker (P-12). An expression of numerals alone folds exactly before the checker into a signed constant, which defaults as an integer numeral, as a `QQ` for a quotient, or as an `RR64` with a radix point: so `x = 7/2` is a `QQ`, `1/2 = 0.5` is `true`, and `-2147483648`, prefix minus on a numeral, folded, is a `ZZ32`. `gg(5, w)`, with `gg` declared at `(ZZ32, T)` and at `(Any, Any)`, runs the `ZZ32` declaration (P-49). Why: the design completes the Working Draft's, whose numerals have types of their own so that a library decides by coercions how they meet other types (P-174 gives that ground). It bends P-26 in two places, the numeral types, whose names and static parameters are the Draft's (P-174), and the places that fix no type, where the compiler's prelude, the team's later word, keeps the numeral's own type; the curator takes the bend by his right to finish the specification. He declined exact defaults, `ZZ` and `QQ` (the 2012 restart gives `NN` and `QQ`, `literals.tick:25`), since microGPT's untyped hyperparameters (`MicroGptFlat.fss:18`) would then be rationals and every untyped index would need `asZZ32`. The Draft's text that it contradicts, for Appendix I: numerals "not directly converted" and "polymorphic" (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:132-141`) at places that fix no type, and a `nat` parameter usable wherever an `NN32` variable is (`basic/trait-parameters.tex:84-86`); P-174 names the binary chapter's, which it leaves out. What it keeps and changes in form: the judgement's section 3, but for the numeral types' form, which P-174 holds. Detail: `reviews/literal-design-judgement-2026-11-08.md`, sections 1 to 6 and the 42 test programs of its appendix; the prototype, `compile-ladder/plan-29/probes/literal-default.md`.
  from: coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-29b.md, "For the record-landing workflow"; from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), cut only: "or is a numeral type" went, since P-174 writes a numeral type only as a coercion's or an operator's parameter type and the landed checker refuses a binding at one on both paths (the review's correction A)

- P-174 **A numeral's type exists only at compile time: the Working Draft's `NaturalNumeral` or, with a radix point, `RadixPointNumeral`, below `Numeral`, with its four numbers as static parameters; every number type, the library's too, takes a numeral through a coercion generic over the four numbers.** The four numbers are the digit count `n`, the digits after the radix point `m`, the radix `r` and the digits read as one integer in that radix `v` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:83-129`), so `3.125` is a `RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]`; the four are naturals of any size, and read as values in a coercion's body they are exact integers, `ZZ`; this implementation's `NN32` bound (P-40) is for sizes. The coercion's body calls the type's constructor on values computed from the four numbers, as in `coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]) = Interval(roundDown(v, r, m), roundUp(v, r, m))`, and the compiler instantiates it for the numeral and folds it (P-176). No numeral value exists when a program runs: a numeral type has no getters, no `asFloat`, no `asString` and no `=` with a number type, and is written only as a coercion's or an operator's parameter type. A numeral has no sign: at a program's type `-3` is the type's own prefix `-` applied to the coercion's result for `3`, and a type without `-` refuses it; any other expression of numerals alone folds first (P-173) and reaches such a type only through its coercion from the folded value's type, such as `QQ` for `1/2`, since coercions do not chain. The design leaves out the binary chapter's numeral coercion and `opr ||` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/binary.tex:1620-1662`). Why: the Draft gives numerals types of their own so that "library designers [can] decide how numerals should interact with other types of objects by defining coercion operations", and has libraries define the coercions into integers and rationals (`literals.tex:132-148`); the curator finishes that design, in place of the implementations' `IntLiteral` (P-76) and `FloatLiteral` (P-172). Rows 960 and 974 hold where the tree departs: the digit count of `00.5`, and walk running a numeral type written as a static argument or as a type that an object or a trait extends.
  from: coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-29b.md, "For the record-landing workflow"; from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): its last sentence named rows 969 and 970, the run-time numeral with its four numbers as fields, both FIXED by a26e74f5c8 (c6ef2f0b80); it now names the open rows that hold the decision's departures (the review's correction A)

- P-176 **A numeral at a place denotes one value, the same on both paths and made once: the compiler folds the coercion instantiated for it (P-174), and walk runs that coercion at the place's first evaluation.** Where the result is a JVM `int`, `long`, `float` or `double`, the class file holds it as a constant; otherwise, as at `QQ` or a program's own type, the constructor call on the constants runs once per place, into a `static final` field filled when the class loads, and a program's type's own `-` on the numeral (P-174) runs there too. Walk fixes the coercion's static arguments from the numeral's four numbers and keeps the result for the place; it refuses a numeral only when the place runs, until it runs the checker (P-12). Before the switch-over the compiler's prelude carries this design (P-37), so that both paths give one value at one place. Why: a numeral is a constant of the program, and a JVM class file holds no object, so a field filled once at load is the nearest form of an object constant. Rows 957 and 971 hold where the tree departs: a place whose type names a type parameter, and a coercion that spawns a task.
  from: coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-29b.md, "For the record-landing workflow"; from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): its last sentence named rows 969 and 970, both FIXED by a26e74f5c8 (c6ef2f0b80); the departures are now rows 957, its second case, and 971 (the review's correction A)

- P-76 **An integer numeral has the Working Draft's type `NaturalNumeral` (P-174), which each of the eight number types converts from: exactly into the five integer types and `QQ`, and with one rounding into `RR64` and `RR32`.** Where nothing fixes a type it is a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit (P-173). `NaturalNumeral` takes the place of `IntLiteral`, the name that the compiler's prelude and the interpreter's library gave the type, and `IntLiteral`'s own arithmetic goes, since an expression of numerals alone folds before the checker (P-173). Walk, before it runs the checker (P-12), converts an integer numeral from its exact value at each place that fixes a type, and elsewhere evaluates it at its default, which walk already gives it by its value (F-74). Walk gaps: coercion through tuple and varargs parameters (rows 395 and 511).
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): "give" is now "gave", since a26e74f5c8 deleted the prelude's trait IntLiteral and neither library declares it (ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:886-890 holds the five numeral traits; the review's correction A)

- P-172 **A numeral with a radix point has the Working Draft's type `RadixPointNumeral` (P-174): `QQ` converts from it exactly, and `RR64` and `RR32` with one rounding.** So `3.125` converts to the `QQ` value `3125/1000`, `q: QQ = 0.5` binds and `1/2 = 0.5` is `true`; into `RR64` or `RR32` a numeral rounds once, to the type's nearest value, on both paths. Where nothing fixes a type the numeral is an `RR64` (P-173): `Cell(0.5)` is a `Cell[\RR64\]`, and a bare numeral prints as that double, so `"" 2.50` is `"2.5"`, and the team's `tests/NumberPrintTest.fss:16`, which asserts that a numeral prints as written, changes. `RadixPointNumeral` stands beside the number types, as the compiler's prelude declares it (`LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:890`), in place of the interpreter library's `value object FloatLiteral extends RR64` (`FortressBuiltin.fsi:44` at `b430f9f42`). Walk converts such a numeral from its exact value where its place fixes a type, at a binding, a parameter, a field and a declared return type, so `y: RR64 = 0.5` is `SEQV` a computed `0.25 + 0.25`. The specification's literals chapter and the coercions of `basic-lib/numbers.tex:43` follow, with an Appendix I entry, in the revision form (P-96). A numeral at `round`, `floor`, `ceiling` or `truncate` converts into `RR64` by P-173's reading of a tie among `QQ`, `RR32` and `RR64`, so one within half an ulp of a tie rounds as its nearest double, not as the rational: `round(2.50000000000000001)` is 2. No line of microGPT changes, and by reading its numerals keep their bits. Why: the Working Draft keeps a numeral unconverted, since "converting it immediately to a floating-point number may lose precision", gives 3.125 "the rational value 3125/1000", has the library coerce a compound numeral to the rationals, and converts a static rational expression into a float with "at most one floating-point rounding error" (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:132-173`); the curator reads it so: nothing is lost in `QQ`, and a float of fixed size takes one rounding.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: made true of the landed tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): the prelude declares RadixPointNumeral at CompilerBuiltin.fss:890, and its FloatLiteral at :996 is deleted (a26e74f5c8); "which holds the numeral's exact value" cut, since the numeral's four numbers are static parameters and no numeral value exists at run time (P-174; the review's correction A)

- P-165 **`asFloat` and the `=` built on it leave `trait Number`; each built-in number type keeps its own `asFloat`.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR32` and `RR64` each declare `asFloat`, so the written conversion `asFloat(x)` into `RR64` is unchanged at every concrete type; the numeral types declare none, since no numeral value exists when a program runs (P-174). Two numbers of different types compare by `=` only where a coercion carries one into the other (P-74); otherwise the program writes the conversion, `asFloat(x) = y`. The vector norm takes a bound that promises a float; its form is the default of a fork on his review list: the norm at each of the eight number types, each by its own `asFloat` (F-324). `Float`'s `^` at a `Number` exponent, `random`, `atan2` and `printThreadInfo` are declared at `RR64`, and coercion carries `ZZ32`, `RR32` and numerals to them. The specification's sentence follows (`Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex:58-75`). P-74's rule, an exact conversion implicit and a lossy one written, stands. Why: the team's compiler prelude declares `Number` with nothing in it (`LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:509`), and the specification does not ground the method: its sentence that names it on `Number` is the revival's revision. The curator's rule for such a case: an early idea of the interpreter's library goes where the specification does not ground it and the later prelude eliminated it. With `Number` open (P-160), an abstract `asFloat` would force a program's own number, such as a complex number, to invent a float, and `Number`'s `=` would compare two different complex numbers by it.
  from: compile-ladder/climb-batch-29b/RECORD.md, "Left for the coordinator and the skill writer"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b moved, re-anchored under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): numbers.tex by rung T, CompilerBuiltin.fss by rung A (the review's correction A)

- P-171 **Two string literals of one text are one object, on both paths, and a concatenation of two string literals is folded into one literal.** P-168's rule stands: strings are reference objects, and their text equality is their own `=`. So `"ab" SEQV "ab"` is `true`, also for one literal evaluated twice, and so is `("foo" || "bar") SEQV "foobar"`, by the fold that `Specification/basic/expressions/constant.tex:129-133` names; a string built at run time is not `SEQV` an equal literal, unless Java answers the same object, as `substring(0, length)` answers the string itself, and then both paths answer `true`. Walk interns each literal's text with `String.intern()` as its evaluator makes the string, and its `SEQV` compares two flat strings' carried Java strings by `==`. The compiled path keeps loading each literal with `ldc`, which yields the JVM's interned `String` (JVM specification, 5.1), and `equality.sEquiv` compares the Java strings that two `FJavaString` wrappers carry by `==`, not the wrappers. The specification gains, in the revision form (P-96), a sentence that a string literal denotes one object and one on the folded concatenation. The team's test `compiler_regressions/seqv.fss:22`, `"cat"==="cat"`, passes on the one library as written. Why: every team source that speaks of literals makes equal literals one value: the specification's "Literals are values; they do not require evaluation" (`literals.tex:25-26`), the 2012 restart's "All literals represent value objects" (`Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/Expressions/literals.tick:29`), the team's code of 2008 and 2010, and that test; and Java, Kotlin, Scala and Clojure, whose strings are Java strings, give equal literals one object. Detail: `reviews/literals-2026-11-08.md`, sections 6.1 and 6.4, way S-b with its compiled way (i), and way S-d.
  from: compile-ladder/climb-batch-29b/RECORD.md, "Left for the coordinator and the skill writer"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that rung T of climb batch 29b moved, re-anchored under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"; the review's correction A)

- P-163 **`Char`'s `asExprString` writes, for every code point, a text that reads back as the same character: the compiled helper's form, completed.** The helper `nativeHelpers/simpleChar.charToExprString` escapes `"` and `\` as `'\"'` and `'\\'`, writes a printable character as itself with its code, the redundant form `'é 00e9'` that Steele built in 2011, and writes the code alone where the parser cannot read the character: the controls, U+201C, U+201D, U+2028, U+2029 and the surrogates. Walk's binding (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:594`) names that helper, as P-58 asks of every native binding, so both paths write the same text. The parser tries its longer choices of hexadecimal digits first (`parser/Literal.rats:460-466`), so that a six-digit code reads back. The specification's list of character-literal forms (`Specification/basic/lexical-structure.tex:860-891`) is revised in the revision form (P-96) to name the redundant form, which the parser has read since 2011, with an Appendix I entry. Why: the library's contract, the team's comment "will equal self", holds at every code point only with the code alone or with this form; this form is the team's later word, Steele's helper and the parser rule that he wrote with it in one commit, with his own two escape cases reached; and the peers write a printable character as itself, where the code alone hides it. The compiled path's text changes at about 2K code points and walk's at every code point outside printable ASCII, and no library code, suite test or model line calls a `Char`'s `asExprString`. Detail: `reviews/char-asexprstring-2026-11-06.md`, sections 1, 6 (d), 7 and 8.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: a line citation that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)")

- P-166 **`ZZ`'s and `QQ`'s values are value objects, and the specification's list of reference objects stops naming arbitrary-precision numbers.** The library declares `value object BigNum extends ZZ` (`LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:544`, `.fsi:196`), so every `ZZ` value is a value object, and `QQ`'s object `Ratio` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:648`) is a `value object` too. By the specification's rule two value objects of one type are strictly equivalent when their fields are, so two `ZZ` values built apart are `SEQV` when their integers are equal, `SEQV` converting each into its bits and comparing those, and two equal rationals built apart are `SEQV` in the same way. The specification's list in `Specification/basic/objects.tex` ("Reference objects include arbitrary-precision numbers") is revised to name them among the value objects, in the revision form (P-96) with one Appendix I entry. Why: the library's `value` declaration is the deliberate word, and where it is, the curator's rule brings the specification's example in line with the code. Detail: `reviews/any-carrier-2026-11-06.md`, section 2.4, point 11, which found the split.
  from: reviews/batch-29b-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a2be3e9e1; note: line citations that climb batch 29b's hunks moved, re-anchored under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)")

- P-175 **On the compiled path a call's arguments run in parallel where `ParallelismAnalyzer` judges them worth a task, as a tuple's elements do, so a reduction's two halves run in parallel; the one library's parallel range stops its split at a grain size, not at single elements.** The one library's range joins a reduction's halves as the arguments of one call, `red.join(loop'(lo, mid-1), loop'(mid, hi))`, and splits down to single elements (`Library/RangeInternals.fss:1308-1342`); the code generator evaluates a call's arguments in order (F-19, row 951), under the team's comment "Why isn't this parallel???" (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:7068`), so every `SUM`, `DOT` and `BIG MAX` of microGPT would run its bodies one at a time once compiled programs run the one library. The split stops at a chunk that runs efficiently in sequence: in his words, "you need the right forking constants", as Steele's parallel trees end in sequential leaves and Clojure's persistent vectors take their branching factor from B-trees. It needs a group of forked arms to join every arm before it completes abruptly, which the compiled path does not do (`CodeGen.genParallelExprs` joins on the normal path only, row 947; `reviews/parallelism-foundations-2026-11-08.md`, section 7.1). The library binding the halves as a tuple, and reductions left in sequence, are declined. Why: the specification evaluates each argument of a call in an implicit thread of its own (`Specification/basic/evaluation/parallelism.tex:60-64`), walk forks a call's arguments (`interpreter/evaluator/Evaluator.java:342`), and the model's parallelism is its reductions (the review, section 6). Detail: the review, sections 1.8, 6, 7.1 and 8.
